    "name": "default-v1",
    "description": "The Analyst Profile MISP ships with, and the one every value page is scored by until somebody enables another. It holds the judgements the assessment reads: what each kind of evidence is worth, where those points become a lean and a quality band, what evidence is left out of the count, how long a report stays current without corroboration, how far each source is trusted, and which enrichment modules are offered for a value of each type. It is meant to be forked rather than edited: an organisation or an analyst takes a copy, changes their own, and the nearest profile to the reader wins. The two reference maps ship empty - no organisation is graded and no warninglist is re-categorised - so trust weighting stays off until somebody fills them, and the profile then scores exactly as it would with no reference section at all. The enrichment mapping names twelve modules across the thirteen attribute types an analyst meets most, preferring CIRCL's services where one exists, and it does three things carefully. It runs nothing: a named module arrives ticked on the Enrichment tab and a run still takes a press. It enables nothing: a module an administrator has not turned on stays off, and the editor says so. And it only adds ticks: a module this profile does not name arrives unticked and is still there to tick by hand.",
